This approach has a space complexity of **O(1)** (ignoring the space used by the sorting algorithm) and a time complexity of **O(N log N + M log M)**.

### The Logic:
1. **Sort** both arrays.
2. Use two pointers (`i` for array A, `j` for array B).
3. Compare elements at the pointers:
   - If `A[i] == B[j]`, we found a common element. Print it and increment both pointers.
   - If `A[i] < B[j]`, increment `i` to look for a larger value.
   - If `A[i] > B[j]`, increment `j` to look for a larger value.

### Implementation (Java)

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class CommonElements {
    public static void findCommon(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
        // 1. Sort both arrays
        Arrays.sort(arr1);
        Arrays.sort(arr2);

        int i = 0, j = 0;

        System.out.print("Common elements: ");

        // 2. Use two pointers to traverse
        while (i < arr1.length && j < arr2.length) {
            if (arr1[i] == arr2[j]) {
                // Found a match
                System.out.print(arr1[i] + " ");
                i++;
                j++;
            } else if (arr1[i] < arr2[j]) {
                // Move pointer of the smaller element forward
                i++;
            } else {
                j++;
            }
        }
    }

